> Adaptation has economic benefits beyond avoided losses. The most common co-benefit of adaptation identified in the literature review is the reduction in future increases in borrowing costs due to climatic shocks. According to Cevik et al. (2020), a 1% increase in climate change vulnerability leads to a 3.11% increase in long-term government bond spreads in emerging market economies, while a 1% increase in climate change resilience reduces bond spreads by 0.75%. Early adoption of adaptation strategies has a beneficial impact on the debt-to-GDP ratio because it enables countries to restore their capital stock more quickly after climatic shocks (Catalano et al., 2020). Moreover, adaptation strategies reduce credit rating downgrades, which also has positive fiscal impacts for economies (Klusak et al., 2023).
> 
> Standard Chartered (2023) estimates that co-benefits of adaptation related to GDP boost account for 3.8% of the total benefits of adaptation investment at the national level across 10 market economies in a 1.5°C warming scenario. According to WRI (2025), co-benefits exceed avoided losses in all sectors of the economy when considering not only GDP boost but also social and environmental benefits of adaptation, which include GHG emission reductions, ecosystem services restoration, well-being benefits and health improvements. 
> 
> Adaptation investments can yield substantial returns, particularly when considering the broad co-benefits and opportunities created, although economic returns often surpass financial ones. 
> 
> The effectiveness of labour reallocation to less weather-exposed economic activities as an adaptation strategy compared to physical adaptation is uncertain. Productivity losses from climate shocks may be offset if capital and labor reallocate to less weather-exposed activities (World Bank, 2024a). Nevertheless, the evidence is contradictory. Rode et al. (2022) find that in an RCP8.5 scenario, the global labor disutility costs of climate change in 2099 when there is no adaptation but there is a change in workforce composition toward less weather-exposed economic activities is 1.5%. This value increases by 0.3 percentage points when physical adaptation is adopted, meaning that labor reallocation has a greater impact than physical adaptation strategies in avoiding labor disutility losses.
> 
> Even though in the literature review it was found that rising temperatures is the most common driver of risk studied in macro and fiscal reports, adaptation strategies studied at a national level focus on infrastructure solutions to mitigate capital loss associated with sea-level rise and extreme weather events. Nevertheless, this preventive adaptation is limited as there are more damages (Agrawala et al., 2010). The limited evidence at a national level on cooling systems is not concluding. For firms, the implementation of cooling systems has a medium damage reduction (EDHEC Climate Institute, 2025) and in India the implementation of climate control technology did not reduce absenteeism of manufacturing workers (Somanathan et al., 2021). Nevertheless, other evidence finds that in the DRC cooling systems increase labour productivity (World Bank, 2023d) and technological climate control reduces the effects of climate change (World Bank, 2024a).
> However, evidence from the Sahel region suggests that the difference in avoided GDP losses between the low, medium, and high growth scenarios, which consider a shift in economic activities from agriculture to industry and services sectors, is not as significant as when there is no adaptation. For example, by 2030 in a dry scenario for 2050, avoided losses in Burkina Faso are expected to be 2.7% of GDP in a low-growth scenario, while in a high-growth scenario they are 2.2% of GDP (World Bank, 2022). Moreover, a systematic quantitative literature review by the World Bank (2024a) found that labor reallocation, mostly through migration and off-farm work, reduces climate losses by only 14%. Studies on the constraints of spatial and sectoral reallocation are also very limited.
> 
> Adaptation strategies are more effective at reducing climate change damages when GHG emissions are reduced worldwide. Climate damages are highest when neither adaptation nor mitigation strategies are implemented globally. Damages decrease substantially when either adaptation or mitigation strategies are adopted independently, with both approaches yielding approximately equivalent reductions. However, climate damages as a percentage of global GDP are lowest when adaptation and mitigation strategies are combined and implemented simultaneously (Agrawala et al., 2010). 
> 
> There are abundant opportunities for adaptation, decarbonization, and development to effectively work together, by overcoming challenges related to coordination and market dynamics, and thereby crowd in private finance. For example, Brazil is showing that investing in nature-based solutions (NbS) offers a sustainable and fiscally beneficial approach for countries to enhance climate resilience and economic growth by integrating ecosystem management with development strategies (see Box 3).
